      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "Bipolarity is the Toronto Blue Jays' defining trait this July, a team that can blank an opponent then get utterly dismantled in the same night. <strong>The Jays fell to the Mariners 11-0</strong> on July 4, a disaster fueled by Randy Arozarena's grand slam and a horrific Shane Bieber outing that left Toronto with just one hit. Yet, just hours earlier, they had blanked Seattle 2-0, thanks to Dylan Cease's seven scoreless innings - a stark reminder of their 42-47 record and 3rd-place AL East standing, 11.5 games behind the leader. The narrative swings wildly: Cease is a franchise record-setter with 137 strikeouts, while Bieber's collapse underscores the pitching fragility that keeps them in the hunt but not atop it. <strong>Tonight at 5:00 PM ET, the finale against the Mariners looms as a desperate bid for a series win</strong>, with Trey Yesavage taking the ball to salvage what's left of this volatile stretch.",
        "updated": "Updated July 5, 2026"
      },
